This project shows how to build a complete computer system step by step.
We install a Linux server, create users and control what each one can do,
set up virtual machines and networks, allow safe remote access, and scan
the system to find security problems. We also explain the common problems
that can happen at each step and how to fix them.

                               LINUX

1. build a small Linux server inside a virtual machine, so anyone can do
it again:
    We have installed a minimal version of Debian. This has no graphical 
    interface. We divide the disk into different sections. We  have:
    a section for /, a section for /home, a section for /var, a section
    for /swap (extra RAM)
2. Making a disk part bigger:
    We can increase the size of a section without deleting any data.
3. network:
    Static IP and nameserver set up. We can check that internet and
    domains are working by running ping.
4. Our own service:
    We created a small script that will check every 2 seconds if a file 
    is exists. If it isn’t, it creates a new file. We have used systemd
    to allow our service to always run. We tested that our service will
    always restart by deleting the file, killing the process and
    restarting the machine. In all cases, the service was restored.
5. Boot analysis:
    We use a command to see which parts of the system start slowly.

                               LOGIN

We set access controls on a common Linux server. Each role (admin,
developper, deployer) only have specific rights to allow them to perform
their task. This follows the principle of least privilige.

What is achieved?

User and Group management: one group created for each role. This means
rights are assigned to the role rather than to individuals.
Sudo: Admin has full rights. Devs and Deployers can only restart the service.
Password policies: Passwords will have an expiry date. If a user attemps to
login incorrectly 3 times, they will be locked out for 10 minutes.
Shared directories: Project directory. Developers can read and write, Deployers
can only read. Scratch directory. All users can write. However, users cannot
delete others files.
Secure SSH: Users must login using key authentication. Password authentication
is disabled.
Testing: A script has been created to attempt to perform disallowed actions to
ensure the rules are working correctly.

                               ADD-VM

This is an automatic rebuild of the previous (Linux server and login) using a
single command: 'vagrant up'. There are no questions to answer and no manual
steps to follow.

We start from a preconfigured minimal Debian machine (a “box”) and configure
it using brief shell scripts. There is one script to build Linux server:
network, DNS and our own service. A second script configures users, groups,
sudo rules, password policy, shared folders and secure ssh. A third script
executes all the security tests. The build will fail if any one test fails.
All scripts can be run multiple times and will have the same result. this 
approch is fast to rebuild. All setting is visible in git.

                               CONNECT

A dual subnet, routed network is created using code.
Typing ‘vagrant up’ generates 3 virtual machines; a gateway and two client
PCs (pc-a and pc-b). The gateway allows communication between the subnets.
It provides internet connectivity using NAT. It also has DHCP and DNS (provided
by dnsmasq). The gateway has an nftables firewall which by default denies all 
traffic. It permits ICMP and one service (SSH from subnet A to subnet B).